Building hecate-ng
==================

Prerequisites (all platforms):
  - Rust toolchain (rustup recommended): https://rustup.rs
  - A C/C++ compiler with OpenMP support (GCC or Clang with libomp)

Native Linux build
------------------

Install dependencies (Debian/Ubuntu):

    apt install build-essential libgomp1

Build:

    cargo build --release

The binary is at target/release/hecate.

Native macOS build
------------------

Install dependencies:

    brew install libomp

If using Apple Clang (the default), the openmp-sys crate should find
libomp via Homebrew automatically.  With GCC from Homebrew, OpenMP
support is built in.  However, some users reported difficulties
with compiling.  OpenMP support is not optional and thus macOS is
currently not a primary target platform.

Build:

    cargo build --release

Cross-compiling for Windows (from Linux)
-----------------------------------------

Install the MinGW-w64 cross-compiler:

    apt install gcc-mingw-w64-x86-64 g++-mingw-w64-x86-64

Add the Rust target:

    rustup target add x86_64-pc-windows-gnu

The .cargo/config.toml already sets the linker for this target.
Build with the following environment variables:

    OPENMP_STATIC=1 \
    LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/lib/gcc/x86_64-w64-mingw32/$(ls /usr/lib/gcc/x86_64-w64-mingw32/ | head -1) \
    CC_x86_64_pc_windows_gnu=x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc \
    CXX_x86_64_pc_windows_gnu=x86_64-w64-mingw32-g++ \
    cargo build --release --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu

The binary is at target/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/release/hecate.exe.

OPENMP_STATIC=1 is required because the openmp-sys crate cannot locate
the MinGW shared library during cross-compilation (it probes using the
host's .so suffix rather than the target's .dll).  LIBRARY_PATH points
it to the MinGW libgomp.a.

The resulting .exe is statically linked against libgomp and does not
require any extra DLLs beyond the standard MinGW C runtime.

Running tests
-------------

    cargo test

All tests use the native toolchain and do not require cross-compilation
setup.
